
MoM – Oakbridge Application & Architecture Discussion

Key Discussion Points

1. Architecture Principles
    * Prioritize reusability of existing tables, components and functionality instead of creating new tables for similar business needs.
    * Maintain a common/monorepo architecture to avoid duplicated functionality and isolated applications.
    * Use proper architecture governance to decide whether a feature should extend an existing first-class entity or require a new entity.
2. ToE / Ticket Functionality
    * Use the existing ToE List + Item model for support tickets and similar workflows.
    * Explore per-list templates so different workflows can have different UI while sharing the same underlying data structure.
3. PR, Environments & Testing
    * Define clear local → remote development → staging → production flow.
    * Establish both human stage gates and automated GitHub PR tests/actions before promotion.
    * Ensure features are properly validated in staging before production.
4. LangSmith / LangGraph
    * LangSmith should be the main technical/observability platform for LLM evaluation and knowledge-gap identification.
    * Start scaffolding LangGraph graphs, subgraphs and nodes.
    * Create a common relationship/junction between applications, agents and LangSmith executions to support human-in-the-loop workflows.
5. Knowledge Base & Permissions
    * Implement hierarchical permissions at Knowledge Base → Hierarchy Node → Article levels.
    * Support user-specific View/Create/Edit/Delete/Publish/Archive permissions.
    * Permissions should inherit from higher levels but allow more granular overrides at lower levels.
6. Shared Entities & Components
    * Reduce junction-table sprawl by creating a shared entity/relationship model where appropriate.
    * Common components such as notes, comments, history, QA runs, suggestions, approvals, reactions, notifications, etc. should be reusable across first-class entities.
    * Maintain consistent UI/UX for these shared components across applications.
7. Assets & File Management
    * Define a common Asset → File → File Version structure.
    * Assets should be polymorphic and reusable across goals, knowledge articles and other entities.
    * Establish consistent file/version management and storage architecture across the monorepo.
8. Database & Code Cleanup
    * During migration, identify and remove dead code, unused tables and obsolete proof-of-concept functionality.
    * Consolidate tables where functionality significantly overlaps.
    * Review whether configuration tables such as LLM settings are still required or should be managed through LangSmith.
9. GitHub / Repository Consolidation
    * Multiple GitHub organizations/repositories currently create repository sprawl.
    * Evaluate consolidation/branch-based integration between the Oakbridge R&D repository and the separate Databricks/Azure DevOps repository.
    * Goal is to avoid manual SQL/code copying and provide proper change tracking and merging.
    * Team to discuss internally and propose a repository strategy within the next couple of weeks.

Next Steps

* Team to review the architecture/deck and existing repository structure.
* Assess shared-table/entity, permissions, asset/file and LangGraph requirements.
* Define PR/staging gates and automated testing approach.
* Evaluate GitHub/Databricks repository consolidation options.
* Follow-up discussion to validate understanding and finalize the implementation approach.
